Title: GOOD MOURNING
Credit: Episode 103
Author: Development draft
Draft date: 6 November 2026
Source: Terms and Conditions Apply
Notes: First draft. Target 9:45 story plus 0:15 credits. Scene 103.3 intentionally occupies two minutes without dialogue.

# 103.1 | 00:00-01:15

INT. CANTEEN ENTRANCE - THURSDAY AFTERNOON

LIBERATION AFTERNOON hangs over the doorway. Under it, Mrs. Dev clutches an appointment card.

MRS. DEV
I thought this was an appointment.

ISHA
It is. Liberation is optional.

Mara removes the sign. Turns it over. Writes INDEPENDENT APPOINTMENTS.

Nadi sets three plain cards on a table: CONTINUE. ASSESS RELEASE. DECIDE LATER.

MRS. DEV
Will I have to cry?

NADI
No.

MRS. DEV
I can't do it with people watching.

NADI
Then no one will watch.

She opens the screened consultation door so Dev can inspect the space herself.

Len appears in a company jacket, carrying a clipboard too large to be discreet.

LEN
Registered compliance observation.

ISHA
Do you want a coat hook or a conscience?

MARA
He can observe the registration process. Not consultations.

Len nods and closes his clipboard.

LEN
A coat hook would be helpful.

He takes off the jacket before entering.

# 103.2 | 01:15-02:30

INT. CANTEEN WAITING AREA - AFTERNOON

Mrs. Dev emerges holding a new appointment card.

MRS. DEV
I'm continuing.

Isha opens her mouth. Makes it a smile rather than a speech.

ISHA
Tea?

MRS. DEV
Normal tea. Not something cleansing.

Len recognizes her.

LEN
You live by my dad.

MRS. DEV
Your father steals my newspapers.

LEN
He says you finish them.

MRS. DEV
I do. That's not the point.

She shows him the card.

MRS. DEV
The transport won't get there by nine.

Len checks the route timetable on his own phone. Finds the problem. Sits beside her rather than standing over the form.

Across the room, an enthusiastic RESIDENT raises a phone toward the consent desk.

ISHA
Not the forms.

RESIDENT
Just showing people it's working.

ISHA
Show them the kettle. The kettle's agreed to publicity.

The resident lowers the phone. Isha checks that no one is in the image before permitting a photograph of the tea station.

Mara leads Martin toward the quiet kitchen.

MARA
Nadi's with someone. Would you like company while you wait?

He nods.

# 103.3 | 02:30-04:30

INT. CANTEEN QUIET KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

Two chairs. The chipped kettle. A small window onto a wall.

Martin sits. Mara sits at a professional angle, knees together, hands arranged for listening.

Hold this scene for two minutes. Neither speaks. Do not substitute a montage or explanatory voice-over. Small actions occupy actual time.

Martin places his work gloves on the table. One inside the other. Takes them apart. Leaves them separate.

Mara notices an uneven chair leg. Instinctively leans toward it. Stops when the movement makes him turn. She settles back.

A cart passes outside. Someone laughs in the canteen, unaware of this room. Martin hears it without being insulted by its existence.

Mara picks up a pamphlet. Its cover reads WHAT COMES NEXT. She turns it face down before offering it.

The kettle's indicator goes dark. Mara watches it as though a malfunction has given her permission to do something useful.

Martin reaches across and holds down the switch himself. The water begins again.

He gestures to the other cup. Mara brings it closer.

Steam slowly clouds the little window. Neither person looks especially transformed.

Mara draws breath. A reassuring sentence almost starts.

She lets the breath out.

Martin releases the switch. The kettle has boiled. His fingers remain on its handle for a moment, feeling the warmth.

He pours his water, then hers. She doesn't thank him in the particular voice used for patients doing well.

They sit. Two people drinking something too hot to drink quickly.

At the end of the two minutes, Nadi knocks once on the open door.

Martin stands. Mara does not stand until he has gone.

# 103.4 | 04:30-06:00

INT. CANTEEN ACCOUNTS TABLE - LATE AFTERNOON

A printer wakes up. Suri reads the page while it is still warm.

GROUP STABILITY CERTIFICATE: FROZEN PENDING VERIFICATION.

Her finger travels to the date of the next housing payment.

SURI
That's seventeen households.

ISHA
Seventeen patients?

SURI
Households. People who live with patients. Children. People who signed nothing today.

Tessa opens the reserve ledger from the biscuit tin.

TESSA
Thirty credits a household, a day. Five hundred and ten.

MARA
The care fees are current.

NADI
Then this doesn't cancel clinical appointments. But don't tell anyone housing is protected until it is.

Len reads the notice. His clipboard lowers.

LEN
They normally call first.

SURI
To ask permission to stop the rent?

LEN
To ask if the building wants expedited verification.

Mara understands the offer before it comes.

Her phone rings. PELL.

The kettle sits beside it, still warm from Martin's hand.

# 103.5 | 06:00-07:30

INT. PELL'S CONSULTING ROOM / CANTEEN - INTERCUT

Pell has the same freeze notice on his screen.

PELL
This is verification. It isn't a sanction.

MARA
Then resume the payment while you verify.

PELL
Uniform renewal gives us an approved record. We could clear it today.

MARA
Their clinical consent clears their housing?

PELL
Don't phrase it like that.

MARA
How should I phrase it on the appeal?

A beat.

PELL
The group certificate combines several obligations.

MARA
Send me the rule that says they can only meet one by agreeing to another.

PELL
I'll send the policy notice.

MARA
Send it.

He sees, too late, that she intends to keep the answer rather than accept it.

PELL
Mara. People will be frightened.

MARA
They already are.

She ends the call without the customary thank-you.

His written notice arrives. She downloads it, with its date and authority intact.

Suri reads over her shoulder.

SURI
Now it's something a stranger can judge.

# 103.6 | 07:30-08:45

INT. CANTEEN KITCHEN - EVENING

Isha has photographed a stack of appointment pages. Her phone is ready to upload.

MARA
Whose names are those?

ISHA
The people they did it to.

MARA
Did they ask you to show everyone?

ISHA
No one believes a table of numbers.

MARA
Then ask. Not tonight. Not while they're scared.

Isha looks at one page. A familiar neighbor's loss, reduced to two clinical lines.

She deletes the photographs. Opens the recently deleted folder and removes them there too.

ISHA
I hate that you know where all the buttons are.

MARA
Me too.

They construct a smaller packet: Mara's source record, her cancellations, anonymous staffing totals, original contract clause, freeze notice. Each page has a source receipt.

Suri checks every page before submitting the supplement to Mara's existing complaint.

ON SCREEN: SUPPLEMENT RECEIVED. REQUEST FOR EXPEDITED STAY UNDER REVIEW.

No triumphant message. Just a receipt.

# 103.7 | 08:45-09:45

INT. CANTEEN - CLOSING TIME

Len waits until Nadi has put away the last clinical file.

He places a printed operational notice beside Mara's evidence packet.

LEN
This isn't a patient record. I can identify where it came from.

UNPROFITABLE RECOVERY CLUSTER. ADMINISTRATIVE VERIFICATION HOLD.

At the bottom: CLASSIFICATION FRAMEWORK — M. VENN.

Mara doesn't cover the name.

MARA
I wrote the framework.

Isha sits down very slowly.

ISHA
For this?

MARA
For accounts they called unstable.

ISHA
You mean people.

MARA
Yes.

ISHA
Who else?

Mara takes a clean sheet of paper.

MARA
I don't know yet.

She writes AFFECTED HOUSEHOLDS at the top. Beneath it, nothing.

Len waits. Isha waits. The blank page remains between them.

CUT TO BLACK.

[[09:45-10:00 — End credits without the service chime.]]
